{"id": "7e6359e9-9ff8-4333-9d92-ceaab185e13e", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your EBS setup in us-east-1 for production readiness. That covers volumes, snapshots and backups, encryption, performance configuration, and health signals. I'm running that check now.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_mvHFkJAwd6iIzGTR1Rwerq", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-readiness-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Perform a production-readiness review of Amazon EBS in us-east-1 ahead of a launch next week (today is 2026-11-11). Read-only; do not make changes.",
  "context": "The user asked 'Is my storage production-ready?'. They want a clear verdict plus a prioritized list of gaps. Start with the agent space account 012345678901_1 and also check any other AWS accounts listed in the agent space associations (use list_associations) that are relevant. Focus on us-east-1. Do a focused first pass and return concise findings; suggest deeper areas rather than going exhaustive.",
  "resources": ["EBS volumes", "EBS snapshots", "AWS Backup plans / Data Lifecycle Manager policies", "EC2 instances attached to volumes", "CloudWatch alarms on EBS metrics", "KMS default EBS encryption setting"],
  "timeframe": "Current state, plus the last 14 days of CloudWatch EBS metrics for volumes attached to running instances (look for burst balance depletion, high queue length, throttling/IOPS or throughput saturation, VolumeStatus / impaired checks).",
  "criteria": "Check and report: (1) inventory: counts by volume type/size/state, unattached (available) volumes, and attached-to-which-instance/tags (identify prod vs non-prod where possible); (2) encryption: account-level EBS encryption-by-default status in us-east-1, any unencrypted volumes/snapshots, and KMS key used; (3) backup/recovery: whether volumes have recent snapshots or are covered by AWS Backup/DLM, snapshot age, snapshots of the production volumes, and any public/shared snapshots; (4) performance fit: gp2 volumes that should move to gp3, io1/io2 provisioned IOPS vs actual usage, gp2/st1/sc1 burst-credit risk, volumes near IOPS/throughput limits, instance-level EBS bandwidth limits vs volume demand; (5) resilience/config: DeleteOnTermination on non-root data volumes of prod instances, Multi-Attach usage, volumes in impaired status, single-AZ concentration; (6) monitoring: presence of CloudWatch alarms for burst balance, queue length, volume status, and free space where applicable; (7) cost hygiene: orphaned volumes and old snapshots. Give a verdict (ready / ready with caveats / not ready), then findings grouped by severity (critical, high, medium, low) with specific resource IDs and recommended fixes. Note anything you could not check because of permissions or missing data."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS volumes, snapshots, encryption and performance in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "761f0063-77e4-48e2-8a2f-947942baea47", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Since DLM changes are blocked as mutative, I should report this and instead check backup selections, recovery points, and snapshot permissions, along with CloudWatch metrics for the volumes attached to the two running instances—the prod payment instance and the perf lab instance. I can query these metrics directly with the CloudWatch get_metric_data call rather than needing a separate exploration skill.

I'm listing out the specific volumes attached to those running instances: a gp2 12GB prod volume and a few others including an io2 volume with 20000 iops and an st1 volume. I should also look at snapshot restorable attributes and the protected resources tracked by AWS Backup.

"}], "parent_id": "c23f9c55-7753-4f77-9832-6e3824e5b8f2"}

{"id": "3e63185d-48ab-4380-b19e-8d741827e622", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "That's enough analysis. The gp2 volume vol-0123456789abcdef0_13 is small at 12GB with baseline IOPS around 100 (burstable to 3000), and its read activity is low with burst balance healthy, so no concern there. I'll write up a concise report, noting the only production-tagged instance is i-0123456789abcdef0_9, flagged for payment-processing with high criticality.

Tallying up the fleet: 24 volumes total, split into 7 gp2, 1 st1, 2 io2, and 14 gp3, with 6 currently unattached and all 24 unencrypted. There are 9 snapshots, also unencrypted, with 5 dating back roughly 588 days from an AMI source. The root volume on the production instance is single-AZ, unencrypted gp2 with no snapshots of its own, and the backup plan "Trial" has no selections or protected resources—only a DLM policy covering the eval volume. Unattached volumes add up to about 287 GiB of orphaned storage.

"}], "parent_id": "c23f9c55-7753-4f77-9832-6e3824e5b8f2"}

